	abstract = {Every year, an increasing amount of radio-frequency (RF) spectrum in the VHF, UHF, and microwave bands is being utilized to support new commercial and military ventures, and all have the potential to interfere with radio astronomy observations. Such services already cause problems for radio astronomy even in very remote observing sites, and the potential for this form of light pollution to grow is alarming. Preventive measures to eliminate interference through FCC legislation and ITU agreements can be e†ective ; however, many times this approach is inadequate and interference excision at the receiver is necessary. Conventional techniques such as RF Ðlters, RF shielding, and postprocessing of data have been only somewhat successful, but none has been sufficient. Adaptive interference cancellation is a realtime approach to interference excision that has not been used before in radio astronomy. We describe here, for the Ðrst time, adaptive interference cancellation in the context of radio astronomy instrumentation, and we present initial results for our prototype receiver.},
